course: biology
lesson: 206
title: Parrots: talkers, nutcrackers and long lives
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Parrots are some of the cleverest birds on Earth, and they live in groups where every bird must learn the sounds of its flock.
explain: That is why a pet African grey may say «hello» the moment the phone rings: it has learned that the ring and the word go together, the way you learn the tune of the school bell.
explain: Parrots make their sounds with a voice organ deep in the chest, and their brains have a special area for learning new sounds, which most birds do not have.
explain: A parrot's hooked beak is a nutcracker, a spoon and a third foot all at once, and a big parrot can live sixty years or more, so a parrot may outlive the person who bought it.
fact: Parrots copy sounds using a voice organ in the chest and a brain area for learning sounds; a famous African grey called Alex learned words for colours, shapes and numbers.
fact: Whether parrots understand what they say is still argued; Alex seemed to use some words with meaning, but most pet parrots copy sounds they hear often.
fact: A parrot's hooked beak and strong jaw crack hard nuts and seeds, and big macaws open nuts that people need a hammer for.
fact: Parrots climb with their beak as a third foot, and their feet have two toes pointing forward and two back, so they can grip branches and hold food like a hand.
fact: Big parrots can live over sixty years, and some are passed on in their owners' wills, the papers that say who gets a person's things after they die.
fact: A parrot that is bored, lonely or stressed may pull out its own feathers; parrots need company, toys and puzzles every day.
fact: Real sailors did bring parrots home from tropical voyages, but catching wild parrots for pets is now restricted to protect them.
fact: A cockatoo raises its fan-shaped crest when excited or alarmed, and its call can be heard over a kilometre away; loud calls like this help a parrot flock keep in touch through the forest.
fact: In the Amazon, hundreds of macaws gather at river banks to eat clay, which may give them salt and help with harsh chemicals in some seeds; scientists still argue which reason matters most.
fact: Spix's macaw, the blue parrot of the film Rio, almost vanished from the wild, and some zoo-bred birds have been released in Brazil, it was reported.
fact: The kea is a curious mountain parrot from New Zealand that solves problems, steals food and sometimes pulls rubber off cars.
fact: Many parrots bond strongly with one person and may bite strangers when nervous or jealous; pinned eyes (the dark centres shrinking to tiny dots), raised feathers and an open beak are warnings.
fact: Some parrots move in time to a beat, and a cockatoo called Snowball became famous for dancing; keeping to a beat is rare in animals.
exercise: Why can an African grey say «hello» when the phone rings? | It has learned that the ring and the word go together | It knows exactly who is calling and wants to say hello first | It is answering the phone for its owner | All birds are born knowing that word
exercise: What did the famous parrot Alex learn? | Words for colours, shapes and numbers | How to read whole books | How to speak three languages fluently | How to write its own name
exercise: Do scientists agree on how much parrots understand of what they say? | No, it is still argued | Yes, they understand every word | Yes, they understand nothing at all | No, because parrots cannot make sounds
exercise: What lets a macaw crack nuts that people need a hammer for? | A hooked beak and very strong jaws | Sharp teeth hidden in the beak | Dropping the nuts from high trees | Soaking the nuts in its spit
exercise: How are a parrot's toes arranged? | Two point forward and two point back | All four point forward | Three forward and none back | It has only two toes on each foot
exercise: How does a parrot use its beak when it climbs? | As a third foot to grip branches | As a brake when sliding down | As a hammer to make steps in the bark | It does not, it only uses its wings
exercise: How long can big parrots live? | Over sixty years | About two years | Exactly ten years | Only until they learn to talk
exercise: Why might a parrot pull out its own feathers? | It is bored, lonely or stressed | It is too hot in summer | It wants to grow brighter feathers | It is getting ready to fly south
exercise: Why are pirates often shown with parrots? | Real sailors brought parrots home from tropical voyages | Parrots steered the ships when the captain was asleep below deck | Parrots were used to find treasure | Parrots only live on ships
exercise: Why is catching wild parrots for pets now restricted? | To protect wild parrots | Because wild parrots cannot talk | Because they bite sailors | Because they eat too much
exercise: What does a cockatoo show when it raises its crest? | It is excited or alarmed | It is going to sleep | It is about to lay an egg | It is hungry for clay
exercise: Why do macaws eat clay from river banks? | It may give them salt and help with harsh chemicals in seeds | They are sharpening their beaks on it before they crack nuts | They build nests from the clay they swallow and then bring back up | They mistake it for ripe fruit
exercise: What is a kea? | A curious, clever mountain parrot from New Zealand | A kind of black crow from the deserts of Australia | A flightless parrot from Brazil | A sea bird that eats fish
exercise: Why might a pet parrot bite a stranger? | It bonds with one person and is nervous or jealous of others | It thinks fingers are nuts | It is hungry for meat and wants a taste of whoever comes near | Parrots bite everyone they meet
exercise: What was Snowball the cockatoo famous for? | Dancing in time to music | Saying over a thousand words | Flying across an ocean | Living for two hundred years
exercise: How common is keeping to a musical beat in animals? | It is rare | Every animal can do it | Only fish can do it | Only cats can do it
test: «my nan's parrot says ur late every time i come in, so it knows im late right» What is right? | Not really, it has learned the words go with someone coming in | Yes, it can tell the time by looking at the big clock on the wall | Yes, parrots understand every word | No, parrots cannot learn words at all
test: In Aladdin, Iago the parrot argues and cracks sarcastic jokes. What is true of real parrots? | They can copy words and learn some labels, but they do not hold conversations | They talk in full sentences like people | They cannot make any sounds at all | They only talk to other parrots in English
test: «3 parrots on a perch n one on my shoulder. how many legs is that» | 8 | 16 | 4 | 12
test: «2 macaws and the dog in the garden, how many tails, and no trunks» | 3 | 2 | 4 | 0
test: «gonna buy a big parrot when im 50, it'll be fine» What should someone think about? | The parrot may live over sixty years and outlive the owner | Parrots live only a year or two | Big parrots are fine left alone all day | Parrots need no care when owners get old
test: What would happen to a clever parrot left alone in a small cage with nothing to do? | It could get stressed and pull out its own feathers | It would be perfectly happy on its own | It would learn to read the newspaper at the bottom of its cage | It would sleep all year
test: «hiker's sandwich got nicked by a green parrot in the mountains lol» Which bird was it most likely? | A kea in New Zealand | A penguin | An African grey in a forest | A cockatoo at a clay lick
test: A parrot holds a nut in one foot and nibbles it. What helps it hold the nut? | Two toes forward and two back, like a hand | Sticky pads on its toes | Its long tail wrapped round the nut | Its wings folded over the nut
test: «that cockatoo's got its head feathers stuck up like a mohawk, what's up with him» What is right? | He is excited or alarmed | He has just had a bath | He is moulting all his feathers | He is too cold
test: In the film Rio, Blu the blue macaw cannot fly at first and lives in a bookshop. What is true of Spix's macaws? | They almost vanished from the wild, and some have been released again, it was reported | They are the most common parrot on Earth and live in every big city park in the world | They never lived in the wild at all | They live only in bookshops
test: «why's the parrot screaming its head off, shut it up» Why are some parrots so loud? | Loud calls carry far, so the flock can keep in touch through the forest | Parrots are always in pain | Parrots are deaf and cannot hear how loud they are, so they shout all day | They are copying a fire alarm every time
test: «parrot's eyes going small, feathers puffed and beak open at my mate» What should the mate do? | Back off, these are warning signs before a bite | Grab the parrot quickly | Put a finger right up to its beak so it can smell you | Shout so it calms down
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the vocal capacity of parrots? | Sounds are learned and reproduced by means of a chest voice organ and a specialised region of the brain | Parrots are born possessed of a complete human vocabulary | Speech is produced by the teeth concealed within the beak | Parrots are incapable of producing any learned sound
exam: Identify the principal consideration which ought to precede the acquisition of a large parrot. | The animal may live beyond sixty years and thus outlive its owner | Large parrots invariably perish within two years | Large parrots require neither company nor occupation | The parrot will assume responsibility for its own care
exam: It is given that a captive parrot has commenced removing its own feathers. Ascertain the most probable cause. | Boredom, loneliness or stress | The approach of migration | An excess of company and toys | A natural requirement for brighter plumage
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the consumption of clay by wild macaws. | Clay may supply salt and counteract harsh chemicals in certain seeds | Clay is consumed solely to sharpen the beak | Clay is mistaken by the birds for fruit | Macaws do not consume clay under any circumstances
